WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.356 --> 00:15.076 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Yellen's
Press Conference September 17,

00:15.436 --> 00:23.296 align:middle
2014 CHAIR YELLEN.

00:23.936 --> 00:24.986 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:25.966 --> 00:29.346 align:middle
The Federal Open Market
Committee concluded its meeting

00:29.346 --> 00:32.586 align:middle
earlier today and, as usual,

00:33.076 --> 00:35.586 align:middle
released its monetary
policy statement.

00:36.446 --> 00:40.806 align:middle
The Committee also released a
document describing the approach

00:40.876 --> 00:44.256 align:middle
the Committee intends to
take when, at some point

00:44.256 --> 00:46.976 align:middle
in the future, it
becomes appropriate

00:47.046 --> 00:49.706 align:middle
to begin normalizing
the stance of policy.

00:50.916 --> 00:52.936 align:middle
Let me underscore
that our release

00:52.936 --> 00:57.046 align:middle
of this information is not
meant to convey any change

00:57.046 --> 00:58.526 align:middle
in the stance of policy.

00:59.556 --> 01:03.606 align:middle
As you know, the FOMC's
views on policy are conveyed

01:03.606 --> 01:09.066 align:middle
in the policy statement, which
I will now discuss before coming

01:09.106 --> 01:11.286 align:middle
back to our normalization plans.

01:11.726 --> 01:17.106 align:middle
As indicated in our policy
statement, the FOMC decided

01:17.136 --> 01:21.136 align:middle
to make another reduction in
the pace of its asset purchases.

01:21.956 --> 01:25.526 align:middle
The Committee also maintained
its forward guidance regarding

01:25.526 --> 01:29.886 align:middle
the federal funds rate target
and reaffirmed its view

01:30.216 --> 01:32.176 align:middle
that a highly accommodative
stance

01:32.176 --> 01:34.616 align:middle
of monetary policy
remains appropriate.

01:35.766 --> 01:38.276 align:middle
Let me discuss the
economic conditions

01:38.326 --> 01:40.096 align:middle
that underpin these actions.

01:40.576 --> 01:43.246 align:middle
The economy is continuing

01:43.246 --> 01:46.376 align:middle
to make progress toward
the FOMC's objective

01:46.376 --> 01:48.596 align:middle
of maximum sustainable
employment.

01:49.136 --> 01:53.326 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions have improved further

01:53.326 --> 01:54.426 align:middle
in recent months.

01:55.306 --> 01:59.186 align:middle
Although the pace of job growth
has slowed some recently,

01:59.976 --> 02:03.566 align:middle
job gains have averaged
more than 200,000 per month

02:03.566 --> 02:05.166 align:middle
over the past three months.

02:06.246 --> 02:09.866 align:middle
The unemployment rate was
6.1 percent in August,

02:10.596 --> 02:13.886 align:middle
two-tenths lower than the
data available at the time

02:13.886 --> 02:15.676 align:middle
of the June FOMC meeting.

02:17.036 --> 02:20.076 align:middle
Broader measures of
labor market utilization,

02:20.386 --> 02:24.996 align:middle
such as the U-6 measure, have
shown similar improvement,

02:25.326 --> 02:28.626 align:middle
and the labor force
participation rate has

02:28.626 --> 02:29.526 align:middle
flattened out.

02:31.076 --> 02:33.666 align:middle
These developments
continue the trend

02:33.666 --> 02:36.936 align:middle
of gradual progress toward
our employment objective.

02:38.286 --> 02:41.216 align:middle
But the labor market has
yet to fully recover.

02:41.986 --> 02:45.386 align:middle
There are still too many
people who want jobs

02:45.426 --> 02:49.676 align:middle
but cannot find them, too
many who are working part time

02:49.956 --> 02:53.616 align:middle
but would prefer
full-time work, and too many

02:53.616 --> 02:56.506 align:middle
who are not searching
for a job but would be

02:56.506 --> 02:58.456 align:middle
if the labor market
were stronger.

02:59.676 --> 03:02.196 align:middle
As noted in the FOMC statement,

03:02.626 --> 03:06.396 align:middle
"a range of labor market
indicators suggests

03:06.426 --> 03:09.366 align:middle
that there remains
significant underutilization

03:09.366 --> 03:10.806 align:middle
of labor resources."

03:11.336 --> 03:16.416 align:middle
The Committee continues to see
sufficient underlying strength

03:16.416 --> 03:20.056 align:middle
in the economy to support
ongoing improvement

03:20.056 --> 03:20.956 align:middle
in the labor market.

03:22.246 --> 03:25.866 align:middle
Although real GDP rose
at an annual rate of only

03:25.866 --> 03:28.486 align:middle
about 1 percent in the
first half of the year,

03:29.516 --> 03:33.706 align:middle
that modest gain reflected,
in part, transitory factors,

03:33.956 --> 03:36.096 align:middle
including a dip in net exports.

03:37.356 --> 03:41.416 align:middle
Indeed, private domestic
final demand-that is,

03:41.416 --> 03:45.236 align:middle
spending by domestic
households and businesses-grew

03:45.236 --> 03:47.596 align:middle
about twice as fast as GDP.

03:48.996 --> 03:51.226 align:middle
Indicators of spending
and production

03:51.226 --> 03:52.936 align:middle
for the third quarter suggest

03:52.936 --> 03:56.956 align:middle
that economic activity is
expanding at a moderate pace,

03:57.676 --> 04:00.856 align:middle
and the Committee continues
to expect a moderate pace

04:00.856 --> 04:02.216 align:middle
of growth going forward.

04:03.586 --> 04:07.076 align:middle
Inflation has been running
below the Committee's 2 percent

04:07.076 --> 04:11.836 align:middle
objective, but with longer-term
inflation expectations appearing

04:11.836 --> 04:16.196 align:middle
to be well anchored and the
economic recovery continuing,

04:17.026 --> 04:18.856 align:middle
the Committee expects inflation

04:18.856 --> 04:21.476 align:middle
to move gradually back
toward its objective.

04:22.676 --> 04:26.676 align:middle
Moreover, inflation has firmed
some since earlier in the year,

04:26.676 --> 04:29.666 align:middle
and the Committee believes
that the likelihood

04:29.666 --> 04:32.926 align:middle
of inflation running
persistently below 2 percent

04:33.156 --> 04:34.016 align:middle
has diminished.

04:35.166 --> 04:38.166 align:middle
As is always the case, the
Committee will continue

04:38.166 --> 04:41.926 align:middle
to assess incoming data
carefully to ensure

04:41.926 --> 04:43.556 align:middle
that policy is consistent

04:43.556 --> 04:46.996 align:middle
with attaining the
FOMC's longer-run goals

04:46.996 --> 04:50.406 align:middle
of maximum employment and
inflation of 2 percent.

04:51.496 --> 04:53.846 align:middle
This outlook is reflected

04:53.846 --> 04:57.176 align:middle
in the individual economic
projections submitted

04:57.176 --> 05:03.066 align:middle
in conjunction with this meeting
by the FOMC participants, which,

05:03.066 --> 05:05.796 align:middle
for the first time,
go through 2017.

05:07.216 --> 05:10.936 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

05:10.936 --> 05:15.636 align:middle
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy.

05:16.946 --> 05:18.326 align:middle
The central tendency

05:18.326 --> 05:22.426 align:middle
of the unemployment rate
projections is slightly lower

05:22.426 --> 05:28.996 align:middle
than in the June projections and
now stands at 5.9 to 6.0 percent

05:29.446 --> 05:30.756 align:middle
at the end of this year.

05:32.176 --> 05:35.666 align:middle
Committee participants generally
see the unemployment rate

05:36.196 --> 05:40.356 align:middle
declining to its longer-run
normal level over the course

05:40.356 --> 05:46.286 align:middle
of 2016 and edging a bit
below that level in 2017.

05:47.626 --> 05:49.966 align:middle
The central tendency
of the projections

05:49.966 --> 05:56.486 align:middle
for real GDP growth is 2.0
to 2.2 percent for 2014,

05:57.276 --> 05:59.846 align:middle
down slightly from
the June projections.

06:00.996 --> 06:03.486 align:middle
Over the next three
years, the projections

06:03.486 --> 06:07.976 align:middle
for real GDP growth run
somewhat above the estimates

06:07.976 --> 06:09.656 align:middle
of longer-run normal growth.

06:10.526 --> 06:13.736 align:middle
Finally, FOMC participants
continue

06:13.736 --> 06:17.836 align:middle
to see inflation moving
gradually back toward 2 percent.

06:19.116 --> 06:23.076 align:middle
The central tendency of the
inflation projections is 1.5

06:23.076 --> 06:31.206 align:middle
to 1.7 percent in 2014, rising
to 1.9 to 2 percent in 2017.

06:33.026 --> 06:36.526 align:middle
As I noted earlier, the
Committee decided today

06:36.526 --> 06:39.986 align:middle
to make another reduction in
the pace of asset purchases.

06:41.256 --> 06:45.366 align:middle
Two years ago, when the FOMC
began this purchase program,

06:45.686 --> 06:50.136 align:middle
the unemployment rate stood
at 8.1 percent, and progress

06:50.136 --> 06:54.116 align:middle
in lowering it was expected
to be much slower than desired

06:54.506 --> 06:57.096 align:middle
without additional
policy accommodation.

06:58.386 --> 07:00.116 align:middle
The intent of the program was

07:00.116 --> 07:03.836 align:middle
to achieve a substantial
improvement in the outlook

07:03.836 --> 07:06.386 align:middle
for the labor market
and to ensure

07:06.386 --> 07:09.206 align:middle
that inflation was moving
back toward the Committee's

07:09.476 --> 07:11.266 align:middle
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

07:12.566 --> 07:15.736 align:middle
In light of the cumulative
progress toward maximum

07:15.736 --> 07:18.356 align:middle
employment and the
improvement in the outlook

07:18.356 --> 07:21.126 align:middle
for labor market conditions
since the inception

07:21.126 --> 07:24.036 align:middle
of the program, and
with the likelihood

07:24.036 --> 07:27.046 align:middle
of inflation running
persistently below 2 percent

07:27.046 --> 07:30.776 align:middle
having diminished somewhat,
we have reduced our pace

07:30.776 --> 07:33.326 align:middle
of asset purchases
again at this meeting.

07:34.326 --> 07:38.706 align:middle
Starting next month, we will
be purchasing $15 billion

07:38.706 --> 07:42.106 align:middle
of securities per month,
down $10 billion per month

07:42.106 --> 07:43.116 align:middle
from our current rate.

07:44.386 --> 07:47.666 align:middle
If incoming information
broadly supports the Committee's

07:47.666 --> 07:51.826 align:middle
expectation of ongoing
improvement in the labor market

07:51.826 --> 07:54.086 align:middle
and inflation moving back

07:54.086 --> 07:57.186 align:middle
over time toward its 2
percent longer-run objective,

07:57.996 --> 08:01.196 align:middle
the Committee will end this
program at our next meeting.

08:01.756 --> 08:06.956 align:middle
The Committee will continue its
policy of reinvesting proceeds

08:06.956 --> 08:09.736 align:middle
from maturing Treasury
securities

08:10.326 --> 08:14.296 align:middle
and principal payments from
holdings of agency debt and MBS.

08:15.236 --> 08:17.166 align:middle
The Committee's sizable holdings

08:17.166 --> 08:22.126 align:middle
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

08:22.126 --> 08:26.206 align:middle
financial conditions and promote
further progress toward our

08:26.206 --> 08:29.966 align:middle
objectives of maximum employment
and inflation of 2 percent.

08:31.326 --> 08:32.946 align:middle
Regarding interest rates,

08:33.216 --> 08:36.216 align:middle
the Committee reaffirmed
its forward guidance

08:36.466 --> 08:38.566 align:middle
"that it likely will
be appropriate

08:38.566 --> 08:42.206 align:middle
to maintain the current target
range for the federal funds rate

08:42.836 --> 08:47.216 align:middle
for a considerable time after
the asset purchase program ends,

08:47.826 --> 08:50.676 align:middle
especially if projected
inflation continues

08:50.716 --> 08:54.426 align:middle
to run below the Committee's
2 percent longer-run goal,

08:54.996 --> 08:58.096 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain

08:58.096 --> 08:58.866 align:middle
well anchored."

09:00.256 --> 09:03.586 align:middle
This judgment is based on
the Committee's assessment

09:03.586 --> 09:07.696 align:middle
of realized and expected
progress toward its objectives

09:07.906 --> 09:12.586 align:middle
of maximum employment and 2
percent inflation-an assessment

09:12.586 --> 09:17.396 align:middle
that is based on a wide range of
information, including measures

09:17.396 --> 09:19.156 align:middle
of labor market conditions,

09:19.406 --> 09:21.486 align:middle
indicators of inflation
pressures

09:21.726 --> 09:24.416 align:middle
and inflation expectations,
and readings

09:24.416 --> 09:26.056 align:middle
on financial developments.

09:27.146 --> 09:31.376 align:middle
Further, once we begin to
remove policy accommodation,

09:31.696 --> 09:34.836 align:middle
it is the Committee's
current assessment that,

09:34.836 --> 09:36.066 align:middle
even after employment

09:36.066 --> 09:39.266 align:middle
and inflation are near
mandate-consistent levels,

09:39.886 --> 09:43.416 align:middle
economic conditions
may, for some time,

09:43.616 --> 09:47.686 align:middle
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

09:47.686 --> 09:50.276 align:middle
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run.

09:51.436 --> 09:56.206 align:middle
This guidance is consistent with
the paths for appropriate policy

09:56.626 --> 09:59.516 align:middle
as reported in the
participants' projections.

10:00.686 --> 10:05.256 align:middle
As I will explain in a moment,
the FOMC now anticipates

10:05.256 --> 10:09.076 align:middle
that it will continue to
establish a target range,

10:09.626 --> 10:13.216 align:middle
rather than a single point,
for the federal funds rate

10:13.216 --> 10:17.256 align:middle
when normalization
begins, and the dots

10:17.456 --> 10:21.586 align:middle
in the chart I've distributed
now show, for each participant,

10:21.946 --> 10:24.416 align:middle
the midpoint of this
target range.

10:25.546 --> 10:28.436 align:middle
Notably, although
the central tendency

10:28.436 --> 10:34.226 align:middle
of the unemployment rate in
late 2016 is slightly below its

10:34.226 --> 10:38.746 align:middle
estimated longer-run value,
and the central tendency

10:38.746 --> 10:42.246 align:middle
for inflation is close to
our 2 percent objective,

10:43.096 --> 10:46.406 align:middle
the median projection for the
federal funds rate at the end

10:46.406 --> 10:53.896 align:middle
of 2016, at 2.9 percent, remains
nearly a percentage point below

10:53.896 --> 10:57.956 align:middle
the longer-run value
of 3-3/4 percent

10:58.096 --> 11:00.826 align:middle
or so projected by
most participants.

11:02.056 --> 11:06.026 align:middle
Although FOMC participants
provide a number of explanations

11:06.206 --> 11:09.676 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
running below its longer-run

11:09.676 --> 11:14.166 align:middle
normal level at that time,
many cite the residual effects

11:14.166 --> 11:18.426 align:middle
of the financial crisis, which,
although slowly diminishing,

11:18.726 --> 11:22.356 align:middle
are likely to continue to
restrain household spending,

11:22.986 --> 11:27.086 align:middle
constrain credit availability,
and depress expectations

11:27.346 --> 11:29.846 align:middle
for future growth in
output and incomes.

11:30.416 --> 11:33.716 align:middle
As these factors
dissipate further,

11:34.166 --> 11:38.476 align:middle
most participants expect the
federal funds rate to move close

11:38.476 --> 11:42.136 align:middle
to its longer-run normal
level by the end of 2017.

11:43.586 --> 11:48.196 align:middle
Let me reiterate, however, that
the Committee's expectations

11:48.196 --> 11:52.066 align:middle
for the path of the federal
funds rate are contingent

11:52.066 --> 11:53.486 align:middle
on the economic outlook.

11:54.506 --> 11:58.176 align:middle
If the economy proves to be
stronger than anticipated

11:58.176 --> 12:02.586 align:middle
by the Committee, resulting
in a more rapid convergence

12:02.586 --> 12:06.796 align:middle
of employment and inflation
to the FOMC's objectives,

12:07.686 --> 12:10.786 align:middle
then increases in the
federal funds rate are likely

12:10.786 --> 12:15.896 align:middle
to occur sooner and to be more
rapid than currently envisaged.

12:17.036 --> 12:20.636 align:middle
Conversely, if economic
performance disappoints,

12:21.466 --> 12:24.286 align:middle
increases in the federal
funds rate are likely

12:24.286 --> 12:27.236 align:middle
to take place later
and to be more gradual.

12:28.716 --> 12:30.446 align:middle
Let me now turn to our statement

12:30.446 --> 12:34.096 align:middle
on "Policy Normalization
Principles and Plans."

12:35.246 --> 12:38.356 align:middle
This statement is intended
to provide information

12:38.356 --> 12:42.516 align:middle
to the public about the
eventual normalization process;

12:43.406 --> 12:45.986 align:middle
it does not signal a
change in the current

12:45.986 --> 12:48.356 align:middle
or future stance
of monetary policy.

12:49.436 --> 12:52.436 align:middle
As is always the case
in setting policy,

12:52.436 --> 12:56.166 align:middle
the FOMC will determine
the timing and pace

12:56.166 --> 13:01.106 align:middle
of policy normalization so as
to promote its statutory mandate

13:01.106 --> 13:03.526 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

13:04.766 --> 13:08.556 align:middle
Since the crisis, the Federal
Reserve has been providing

13:08.556 --> 13:12.726 align:middle
extraordinary accommodation
using nontraditional tools

13:12.726 --> 13:14.036 align:middle
of monetary policy.

13:15.376 --> 13:19.376 align:middle
The FOMC's intention has
always been to return

13:19.376 --> 13:23.826 align:middle
to a more traditional approach,
and throughout this period,

13:23.826 --> 13:27.516 align:middle
the Committee has been preparing
for the normalization process.

13:28.566 --> 13:32.426 align:middle
In June 2011, the Committee
set out some broad principles

13:32.936 --> 13:35.566 align:middle
and some more specific tactics

13:36.246 --> 13:39.226 align:middle
for how it envisioned
the normalization process

13:39.226 --> 13:40.216 align:middle
to take place.

13:40.776 --> 13:44.856 align:middle
In June 2013, we noted

13:44.856 --> 13:47.466 align:middle
that conditions had
changed significantly

13:47.686 --> 13:53.296 align:middle
in ways not anticipated in June
of 2011, including the size

13:53.296 --> 13:55.716 align:middle
and composition of the
Fed's balance sheet,

13:56.596 --> 14:00.316 align:middle
and that some revision of those
earlier plans was appropriate.

14:01.526 --> 14:05.796 align:middle
The document released today
reflects our updated plans,

14:06.306 --> 14:08.716 align:middle
which, readers of our
minutes will know,

14:08.986 --> 14:13.066 align:middle
have been under discussion for
the last few FOMC meetings.

14:14.436 --> 14:18.776 align:middle
The new approach retains many
broad objectives and principles

14:18.806 --> 14:22.356 align:middle
from the original but also
has some new elements.

14:23.196 --> 14:27.746 align:middle
As was the case before the
crisis, the Committee intends

14:27.746 --> 14:31.926 align:middle
to adjust the stance of monetary
policy during normalization

14:32.576 --> 14:35.766 align:middle
primarily through actions
that influence the level

14:35.766 --> 14:39.906 align:middle
of the federal funds rate and
other short-term interest rates,

14:40.666 --> 14:43.676 align:middle
not through active management
of the balance sheet.

14:45.126 --> 14:48.096 align:middle
The federal funds rate
will serve as the key rate

14:48.096 --> 14:50.306 align:middle
to communicate the
stance of policy.

14:51.666 --> 14:53.366 align:middle
To begin normalization,

14:53.366 --> 14:55.766 align:middle
the Committee will
raise its target range

14:55.796 --> 14:57.056 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

14:58.446 --> 15:02.556 align:middle
The Committee expects that the
effective federal funds rate may

15:02.556 --> 15:07.106 align:middle
vary within the target range,
and could even move outside

15:07.106 --> 15:08.866 align:middle
of that range on occasion,

15:09.656 --> 15:12.586 align:middle
but such movements should
have no material effect

15:12.586 --> 15:16.336 align:middle
on financial conditions
or the broader economy.

15:16.916 --> 15:21.056 align:middle
The primary tool for moving
the federal funds rate

15:21.346 --> 15:24.476 align:middle
into the target range will
be the rate of interest paid

15:24.476 --> 15:26.816 align:middle
on excess reserves, or IOER.

15:27.526 --> 15:31.246 align:middle
The Committee expects that the
federal funds rate will trade

15:31.246 --> 15:36.176 align:middle
below the IOER rate while
reserves are so plentiful,

15:36.396 --> 15:37.956 align:middle
as is the case at present.

15:39.296 --> 15:40.796 align:middle
The Committee also intends

15:40.796 --> 15:45.216 align:middle
to use an overnight reverse
repurchase agreement facility,

15:46.206 --> 15:49.706 align:middle
which, by transacting with a
broad set of counterparties,

15:50.076 --> 15:53.506 align:middle
will help ensure that the
federal funds rate remains

15:53.506 --> 15:54.876 align:middle
in the target range.

15:55.386 --> 15:57.856 align:middle
I would like to emphasize

15:58.226 --> 16:02.596 align:middle
that the overnight RRP
facility will only be used

16:02.596 --> 16:06.216 align:middle
to the extent necessary
and will be phased

16:06.216 --> 16:07.836 align:middle
out when no longer needed

16:07.836 --> 16:09.896 align:middle
to help control the
federal funds rate.

16:11.196 --> 16:15.326 align:middle
In addition, the Committee will
adjust the particular settings

16:15.326 --> 16:17.186 align:middle
of these tools as needed,

16:17.546 --> 16:21.176 align:middle
and could deploy other
supplementary tools as well,

16:21.446 --> 16:25.016 align:middle
to ensure that we achieve
our desired stance of policy.

16:26.356 --> 16:29.846 align:middle
Turning now to our plans
regarding the Fed's balance

16:29.846 --> 16:34.326 align:middle
sheet, the Committee intends
to reduce securities holdings

16:34.326 --> 16:39.156 align:middle
in a gradual and predictable
manner, primarily by ceasing

16:39.156 --> 16:43.476 align:middle
to reinvest repayments of
principal on securities held

16:43.476 --> 16:45.316 align:middle
in the System Open
Market Account.

16:46.876 --> 16:50.196 align:middle
Regarding the timing for
ceasing reinvestments,

16:50.636 --> 16:53.196 align:middle
the Committee now
expects this to occur

16:53.466 --> 16:56.396 align:middle
after the initial increase
in the target range

16:56.396 --> 16:57.756 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

16:59.066 --> 17:03.126 align:middle
The Committee currently does
not anticipate selling agency

17:03.126 --> 17:05.606 align:middle
mortgage-backed securities
as part

17:05.606 --> 17:07.566 align:middle
of the normalization process,

17:08.356 --> 17:11.936 align:middle
although limited sales might
be warranted in the longer run

17:11.936 --> 17:14.966 align:middle
to reduce or eliminate
residual holdings.

17:16.106 --> 17:19.826 align:middle
The timing and pace of such
sales would be communicated

17:19.826 --> 17:21.336 align:middle
to the public in advance.

17:22.526 --> 17:25.796 align:middle
It's the Committee's intention
that the Federal Reserve will,

17:25.796 --> 17:30.486 align:middle
in the longer run, hold no
more securities than necessary

17:31.116 --> 17:35.676 align:middle
to implement monetary policy
efficiently and effectively,

17:36.466 --> 17:39.586 align:middle
and that these securities
will primarily consist

17:39.586 --> 17:41.056 align:middle
of Treasury securities.

17:42.296 --> 17:46.126 align:middle
As I stated earlier,
today's release

17:46.126 --> 17:49.466 align:middle
of the Committee's updated
normalization plans is

17:49.466 --> 17:51.816 align:middle
in no way intended
to signal a change

17:51.816 --> 17:53.766 align:middle
in the stance of
monetary policy.

17:54.886 --> 17:58.066 align:middle
Rather, it is meant simply
to provide information

17:58.066 --> 18:01.516 align:middle
about how the Committee
envisions the normalization

18:01.516 --> 18:04.956 align:middle
process in light of
the changes in economic

18:04.956 --> 18:08.166 align:middle
and financial circumstances
that have occurred

18:08.166 --> 18:12.886 align:middle
since we put forth our original
plans more than three years ago.

18:13.816 --> 18:17.196 align:middle
That said, conditions
could change further,

18:17.526 --> 18:20.816 align:middle
and we will learn about our
tools during normalization.

18:21.866 --> 18:24.666 align:middle
The Committee has agreed
that it is prepared

18:24.666 --> 18:28.266 align:middle
to make additional adjustments
to its normalization plans

18:28.676 --> 18:32.016 align:middle
if warranted by economic
and financial developments.

18:33.206 --> 18:33.926 align:middle
Thank you.

18:34.276 --> 18:35.396 align:middle
Let me stop there.

18:35.396 --> 18:37.276 align:middle
I'll be happy to
take your questions.

18:37.686 --> 18:39.996 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

18:41.876 --> 18:43.376 align:middle
Thank you.

18:45.766 --> 18:48.706 align:middle
Chair Yellen, there was some
debate going into this meeting

18:48.706 --> 18:50.056 align:middle
about the phrase
"considerable time,"

18:50.056 --> 18:51.476 align:middle
and whether it would
remain in the statement.

18:51.986 --> 18:54.086 align:middle
I want to know if you could
tell me just a couple things

18:54.086 --> 18:54.406 align:middle
about it.

18:54.976 --> 18:57.966 align:middle
First, was it debated at
the FOMC as to whether

18:58.006 --> 18:59.136 align:middle
or not it should be included?

18:59.626 --> 19:01.066 align:middle
And, I'm sorry, we've
been over this before,

19:01.066 --> 19:02.956 align:middle
but what does it mean timewise?

19:02.956 --> 19:03.986 align:middle
And that's just two questions.

19:03.986 --> 19:05.446 align:middle
I have just a couple more here.

19:05.946 --> 19:07.916 align:middle
Is the statement a form
of forward guidance?

19:07.916 --> 19:13.276 align:middle
And, finally, how do you square
this idea of a date when you

19:13.276 --> 19:15.146 align:middle
and others on the FOMC
have continuously said

19:15.496 --> 19:18.006 align:middle
that you're data dependent
to the point where,

19:18.006 --> 19:20.106 align:middle
if the data were to turn,

19:20.606 --> 19:22.556 align:middle
would it not necessarily
be a considerable time

19:22.556 --> 19:23.346 align:middle
until you raise rates?

19:23.346 --> 19:23.636 align:middle
Thank you.

19:23.806 --> 19:24.186 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

19:25.146 --> 19:29.716 align:middle
So, of course, the Committee
discussed its forward guidance

19:29.776 --> 19:35.716 align:middle
today, and it discusses what the
appropriate forward guidance is

19:36.346 --> 19:37.586 align:middle
at every meeting.

19:38.456 --> 19:41.756 align:middle
This is part of our assessment
of economic conditions

19:41.756 --> 19:45.406 align:middle
and the appropriate
stance of monetary policy.

19:50.026 --> 19:54.886 align:middle
In terms of what the term
"considerable time" means,

19:55.476 --> 19:58.516 align:middle
the Committee decided that,
based on its assessment

19:58.516 --> 20:00.216 align:middle
of economic conditions,

20:00.556 --> 20:03.426 align:middle
that characterization
remains appropriate,

20:03.696 --> 20:05.556 align:middle
and it was comfortable with it.

20:06.356 --> 20:10.576 align:middle
I think if you look, for
example, at the projections

20:10.576 --> 20:15.546 align:middle
of individual participants that
are revealed in the SEP-well,

20:15.546 --> 20:19.426 align:middle
that's the view of each
participant, and, again,

20:19.426 --> 20:26.486 align:middle
I'd emphasize, not a Committee
collective view-there is

20:26.486 --> 20:31.316 align:middle
relatively little change in
the assessment of the outlook

20:31.316 --> 20:35.466 align:middle
by participants between
this meeting

20:35.466 --> 20:37.396 align:middle
and the assessment in June.

20:37.906 --> 20:42.726 align:middle
So the outlook is little
changed-a slight decline

20:42.726 --> 20:46.406 align:middle
in the anticipated path
of the unemployment rate

20:46.716 --> 20:50.616 align:middle
and a very slight uptick in
the inflation projection,

20:50.616 --> 20:52.366 align:middle
but really quite minimal.

20:52.906 --> 20:57.276 align:middle
So, the outlook hasn't
changed that much from June,

20:57.276 --> 20:59.696 align:middle
and the Committee
felt comfortable

20:59.696 --> 21:01.336 align:middle
with this characterization.

21:02.196 --> 21:07.176 align:middle
Now, you said, "Isn't this
calendar-based guidance?"

21:07.506 --> 21:12.546 align:middle
I want to emphasize that there
is no mechanical interpretation

21:12.786 --> 21:16.166 align:middle
of what the term
"considerable time" means.

21:16.546 --> 21:21.206 align:middle
And, as I've said
repeatedly, the decisions

21:21.306 --> 21:25.146 align:middle
that the Committee makes about
what is the appropriate time

21:25.146 --> 21:27.216 align:middle
to begin to raise its target

21:27.216 --> 21:30.826 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
will be data dependent.

21:31.386 --> 21:34.526 align:middle
And in my opening
comments just now,

21:34.846 --> 21:38.466 align:middle
I again emphasized something
I've said previously,

21:38.806 --> 21:42.756 align:middle
which is that if
the pace of progress

21:42.756 --> 21:45.216 align:middle
in achieving our
goals were to quicken,

21:45.216 --> 21:46.806 align:middle
if it were to accelerate,

21:47.206 --> 21:51.466 align:middle
it's likely that the Committee
would begin raising its target

21:51.466 --> 21:55.906 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
sooner than is now anticipated

21:56.146 --> 22:01.106 align:middle
and might raise-might then
raise the federal funds rate

22:01.346 --> 22:02.866 align:middle
at a faster pace.

22:02.866 --> 22:04.826 align:middle
And the opposite is also true,

22:05.316 --> 22:07.716 align:middle
if the projection
were to change.

22:08.016 --> 22:13.606 align:middle
So there is no fixed mechanical
interpretation of a time period.

22:14.296 --> 22:17.176 align:middle
I think it would not be accurate

22:17.176 --> 22:20.636 align:middle
to describe the Committee's
guidance about the timing

22:20.636 --> 22:24.946 align:middle
of the federal funds rate and
when it will move above zero

22:25.266 --> 22:27.566 align:middle
as being calendar based.

22:27.916 --> 22:32.446 align:middle
The Committee has started
with a broad general statement

22:32.446 --> 22:36.596 align:middle
of what determines how long it
will keep the federal funds rate

22:36.876 --> 22:38.956 align:middle
target at zero.

22:39.196 --> 22:44.506 align:middle
It has said that it will
be looking at the actual

22:44.506 --> 22:51.236 align:middle
and projected pace at which
the gaps between our employment

22:51.236 --> 22:56.386 align:middle
and inflation, and our goals for
those variables, are closing.

22:56.956 --> 23:04.776 align:middle
And then what the Committee does
at each meeting is-after saying

23:04.776 --> 23:06.136 align:middle
that the assessment will take

23:06.136 --> 23:09.246 align:middle
into account many
different indicators and take

23:09.246 --> 23:12.536 align:middle
into account inflation pressures
and other things-it goes

23:12.536 --> 23:16.616 align:middle
on to provide at that
meeting its assessment

23:16.776 --> 23:21.396 align:middle
of the implications of its
view of the data at that time.

23:21.756 --> 23:25.436 align:middle
And that assessment
really hasn't changed

23:25.656 --> 23:27.416 align:middle
over the last several meetings.

23:27.796 --> 23:30.666 align:middle
The Committee, based on its
assessment at each meeting,

23:30.666 --> 23:34.786 align:middle
has felt comfortable saying
that, based on its assessment

23:34.786 --> 23:37.126 align:middle
of those factors, it considers

23:37.126 --> 23:39.036 align:middle
that it will be likely
appropriate

23:39.256 --> 23:43.056 align:middle
to maintain the current target
range for a considerable time

23:43.056 --> 23:45.556 align:middle
after the asset purchase
program ends,

23:45.846 --> 23:50.026 align:middle
especially if inflation remains
below the 2 percent objective.

23:50.376 --> 23:55.416 align:middle
So I wouldn't describe that as-I
know "considerable time" sounds

23:55.416 --> 24:00.596 align:middle
like it's a calendar concept,
but it is highly conditional,

24:00.596 --> 24:01.336 align:middle
and it's linked

24:01.416 --> 24:04.266 align:middle
to the Committee's
assessment of the economy.

24:04.786 --> 24:06.916 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

24:09.256 --> 24:09.876 align:middle
Thank you.

24:09.876 --> 24:11.116 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

24:11.116 --> 24:11.606 align:middle
Thank you.

24:12.266 --> 24:14.516 align:middle
So if you would help us, I mean,

24:14.746 --> 24:17.106 align:middle
square the circle a little
bit-because having kept the

24:17.106 --> 24:19.426 align:middle
guidance the same,
having referred

24:19.456 --> 24:21.626 align:middle
to significant underutilization
of labor,

24:22.006 --> 24:25.576 align:middle
having actually pushed GDP
projections down a little bit,

24:26.006 --> 24:28.526 align:middle
yet the rate path
gets steeper and seems

24:28.526 --> 24:32.006 align:middle
to be consolidating higher-so
if it's data dependent,

24:32.536 --> 24:36.006 align:middle
what accounts for the faster
projections on rate increases

24:36.006 --> 24:38.116 align:middle
if the data aren't
moving in that direction?

24:38.596 --> 24:39.536 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

24:39.536 --> 24:44.046 align:middle
Well, the growth projections
for 2014 are down a little bit,

24:44.346 --> 24:49.736 align:middle
but the unemployment path
is also marginally lower.

24:49.736 --> 24:55.346 align:middle
So while the projected path of
the labor market-unemployment

24:55.346 --> 24:59.326 align:middle
and other measures of the labor
market-of course is partly

24:59.326 --> 25:03.916 align:middle
dependent on the growth outlook,
it isn't totally dependent

25:03.916 --> 25:05.136 align:middle
on the growth outlook.

25:05.466 --> 25:10.396 align:middle
And the Committee assesses that
the labor market is continuing

25:10.396 --> 25:17.246 align:middle
to improve, and you see a
small reduction in the path

25:17.246 --> 25:20.386 align:middle
of the unemployment this
year and then over the rest

25:20.386 --> 25:21.756 align:middle
of the projection period.

25:22.236 --> 25:25.006 align:middle
So, if you ask me-you asked me,

25:25.316 --> 25:29.546 align:middle
"Why has the projected
funds rate path moved up?"

25:29.546 --> 25:33.496 align:middle
Well, you know, each participant
knows the reason they wrote

25:33.556 --> 25:34.536 align:middle
down what they did.

25:34.636 --> 25:38.136 align:middle
But, as a guess, I would
hazard-first, I would say,

25:38.416 --> 25:42.956 align:middle
there is relatively little
upward movement in the path,

25:43.416 --> 25:46.966 align:middle
and I would view it
as broadly in line

25:47.216 --> 25:52.656 align:middle
with what one would expect with
a very small downward reduction

25:52.656 --> 25:59.566 align:middle
in the path for unemployment
and a very slight upward change

25:59.566 --> 26:01.506 align:middle
in the projection for inflation.

26:01.846 --> 26:06.086 align:middle
So, most participants, in
deciding on the path, I think,

26:06.086 --> 26:10.186 align:middle
look at, as our guidance
says, how large is the gap

26:10.326 --> 26:15.626 align:middle
between performance of the
labor market and that associated

26:15.626 --> 26:19.936 align:middle
with our maximum employment
objective, how large is the gap

26:20.006 --> 26:23.006 align:middle
between inflation and
our 2 percent objective,

26:23.796 --> 26:26.486 align:middle
how fast will those gaps change.

26:26.716 --> 26:32.746 align:middle
And you see in the projections
very modest reductions

26:32.746 --> 26:40.666 align:middle
in the size of those gaps
and modest-very small change

26:40.666 --> 26:45.176 align:middle
of a slightly faster pace at
which those gaps would change.

26:45.436 --> 26:48.116 align:middle
I would describe the
change in the projections,

26:48.586 --> 26:52.876 align:middle
both for the economy and the
path of rates, as quite modest.

26:53.236 --> 26:58.286 align:middle
But the fact that they move does
illustrate the data-dependence

26:58.286 --> 27:01.546 align:middle
principle that I think
is really so important

27:01.546 --> 27:04.656 align:middle
for market participants
to keep in mind,

27:04.926 --> 27:09.196 align:middle
that what we do will depend
on how the data unfold.

27:09.686 --> 27:11.746 align:middle
There is uncertainty about that.

27:12.056 --> 27:16.446 align:middle
And as expectations and
the actual performance

27:16.446 --> 27:19.036 align:middle
of the economy change,
you should expect

27:19.036 --> 27:20.736 align:middle
to see movements in the dots.

27:21.006 --> 27:24.936 align:middle
I think it's also notable
that the further you go

27:24.936 --> 27:29.706 align:middle
out in the projection period,
the wider the set of dots.

27:29.706 --> 27:35.686 align:middle
You see a big range out in 2017,
and that reflects, in part,

27:35.806 --> 27:39.346 align:middle
different forecasts by different
members of the Committee

27:39.346 --> 27:42.046 align:middle
about how rapid progress
will be.

27:42.996 --> 27:44.516 align:middle
What you don't see

27:44.516 --> 27:48.476 align:middle
in the dot-so-called dot
plot is also the uncertainty

27:48.476 --> 27:50.876 align:middle
that each individual,
each participant,

27:51.106 --> 27:53.286 align:middle
sees around their
own projection.

27:53.526 --> 27:58.126 align:middle
So things will depend on
how the economy evolves.

27:58.476 --> 28:02.096 align:middle
That will change over time,
and there's a good deal

28:02.136 --> 28:07.286 align:middle
of uncertainty associated
with it.

28:07.836 --> 28:09.746 align:middle
CHRIS CONDON.

28:10.956 --> 28:11.966 align:middle
Thank you, Madam Chair.

28:11.996 --> 28:13.516 align:middle
Chris Condon, Bloomberg News.

28:14.796 --> 28:17.686 align:middle
Madam Chair, the economy has
been growing now for five years,

28:17.686 --> 28:20.466 align:middle
and some economists believe
the expansion will last another

28:20.546 --> 28:21.346 align:middle
five years.

28:22.226 --> 28:27.896 align:middle
Why, in your view, is economic
growth not creating more

28:28.606 --> 28:32.326 align:middle
inflation in wages and in PCE?

28:32.476 --> 28:36.056 align:middle
Is this all about remaining
slack in the labor market,

28:36.056 --> 28:37.536 align:middle
or are there other
forces at play?

28:38.146 --> 28:38.856 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

28:38.856 --> 28:41.756 align:middle
Well, to my mind,
the very slow pace

28:41.756 --> 28:46.546 align:middle
of wage increases does reflect
slack in the labor market.

28:46.996 --> 28:54.916 align:middle
We had a very deep recession,
as is perhaps to be expected

28:54.916 --> 28:59.606 align:middle
in the aftermath of a very
significant financial crisis.

28:59.926 --> 29:04.506 align:middle
We've faced headwinds in
the economy recovering,

29:04.506 --> 29:07.566 align:middle
so the recovery has been slow.

29:08.626 --> 29:13.856 align:middle
Growth has been positive, and
it's lasted for five years.

29:14.176 --> 29:19.636 align:middle
But it's nevertheless been slow
relative to past recoveries

29:19.636 --> 29:23.736 align:middle
that have not been associated
with financial crises.

29:24.116 --> 29:29.626 align:middle
And while unemployment has
come way down from the slightly

29:29.626 --> 29:35.496 align:middle
over 10 percent level it
reached, at 6.1 percent,

29:35.496 --> 29:41.746 align:middle
it remains significantly
above the level

29:41.876 --> 29:46.326 align:middle
that most FOMC participants
would regard as consistent

29:46.326 --> 29:51.126 align:middle
with normal in the longer
run, 5.2 to 5.5 percent.

29:51.396 --> 29:54.036 align:middle
So there is significant
underutilization

29:54.036 --> 29:55.446 align:middle
of labor resources.

29:55.826 --> 29:59.016 align:middle
We continue to discuss whether

29:59.016 --> 30:04.016 align:middle
or not the unemployment rate
itself is an adequate measure

30:04.016 --> 30:08.936 align:middle
of how much underutilization of
labor resources there really is.

30:09.296 --> 30:12.506 align:middle
And, as I went into
detail in Jackson Hole

30:12.506 --> 30:17.136 align:middle
and won't repeat all of that
there, there are other ways

30:17.136 --> 30:21.106 align:middle
in which we see
underutilization-high levels

30:21.416 --> 30:24.346 align:middle
that have come down
only very marginally

30:24.346 --> 30:27.576 align:middle
of part-time employment

30:27.576 --> 30:30.966 align:middle
for economic-or involuntary
part-time employment,

30:32.126 --> 30:37.646 align:middle
perhaps some remaining shortfall
of labor force participation

30:37.896 --> 30:40.496 align:middle
as a result of cyclical factors.

30:40.906 --> 30:44.596 align:middle
And so, I think there still
is-and the statement says

30:44.596 --> 30:48.956 align:middle
it-"significant underutilization
of labor resources"

30:49.296 --> 30:55.056 align:middle
and a very modest pace of
wage increases that's picked

30:55.056 --> 30:56.676 align:middle
up very little, I see

30:56.676 --> 30:59.626 align:middle
as essentially a
reflection of that.

30:59.706 --> 31:00.376 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

31:02.916 --> 31:05.686 align:middle
Jon Hilsenrath from the
Wall Street Journal.

31:06.566 --> 31:07.746 align:middle
Chair Yellen, I want
to come back

31:07.746 --> 31:10.866 align:middle
to the interest rate projections
that the Fed put out today.

31:11.276 --> 31:13.526 align:middle
The public, I think,
would be enlightened

31:13.526 --> 31:15.656 align:middle
by knowing a bit more
about where you stand

31:15.656 --> 31:17.726 align:middle
in relation to these
projections.

31:18.226 --> 31:21.366 align:middle
And with that in mind, and
in the name of transparency,

31:21.366 --> 31:24.516 align:middle
I wonder if you could
describe to us whether you're

31:24.516 --> 31:26.466 align:middle
at the low end of
those projections,

31:26.496 --> 31:28.636 align:middle
within the central
tendency, or at the high end.

31:29.346 --> 31:33.296 align:middle
I also want to ask you about
a San Francisco Fed paper

31:33.296 --> 31:35.746 align:middle
that came out recently,
which suggested

31:35.746 --> 31:38.786 align:middle
that market expectations have
been running below the Fed's

31:38.786 --> 31:39.966 align:middle
own projections.

31:40.466 --> 31:45.136 align:middle
So I wanted to ask you
if you see that as well,

31:45.136 --> 31:46.326 align:middle
and whether it's
at all troubling

31:46.326 --> 31:48.436 align:middle
that market expectations
might not be aligned

31:48.516 --> 31:50.706 align:middle
with the central-with what
the Fed put out today.

31:50.706 --> 31:51.006 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

31:51.516 --> 31:54.346 align:middle
So, with respect to
identifying myself,

31:54.796 --> 32:00.556 align:middle
the Committee has had
discussions during the years

32:00.666 --> 32:06.146 align:middle
that we have been
providing these forecasts

32:06.146 --> 32:09.486 align:middle
of the participants to
the public as to whether

32:09.486 --> 32:13.016 align:middle
or not it's desirable
from the standpoint

32:13.586 --> 32:17.026 align:middle
of Committee functioning
to identify who's

32:17.026 --> 32:18.966 align:middle
who in these pictures.

32:19.286 --> 32:25.176 align:middle
And thus far, while occasionally
an individual will indicate

32:25.176 --> 32:28.806 align:middle
in a speech what their
personal views are, we have not

32:28.916 --> 32:32.936 align:middle
yet decided that it would be a
desirable thing for the point

32:32.936 --> 32:39.216 align:middle
of view of our decisionmaking
process to identify individuals.

32:40.446 --> 32:42.206 align:middle
We have a subcommittee

32:42.206 --> 32:48.116 align:middle
on communications that's now
chaired by Vice Chair Fischer,

32:48.406 --> 32:53.816 align:middle
and they will be considering
the SEP and whether

32:53.816 --> 32:56.046 align:middle
or not some changes
are appropriate.

32:56.286 --> 33:00.416 align:middle
But until and unless
there is some change

33:00.416 --> 33:06.436 align:middle
in the Committee stance on this,
I don't want to identify myself.

33:07.136 --> 33:12.476 align:middle
You asked, second, about
the San Francisco Fed paper

33:12.536 --> 33:16.346 align:middle
that did point to a notable
divergence between market views

33:16.346 --> 33:18.826 align:middle
and the views of the Committee.

33:19.116 --> 33:23.486 align:middle
I'd say that there have been
any number of different analyses

33:23.886 --> 33:28.486 align:middle
of this topic, there are many
different survey measures

33:28.486 --> 33:31.686 align:middle
and interpretations of
what the market thinks.

33:31.766 --> 33:37.366 align:middle
And I don't frankly think
it's completely clear

33:37.456 --> 33:39.896 align:middle
that there is a gap.

33:39.896 --> 33:42.846 align:middle
There are different
views on whether

33:42.846 --> 33:44.976 align:middle
or not such a gap exists.

33:45.436 --> 33:51.716 align:middle
To the extent that there is a
gap, one reason for it could be

33:51.716 --> 33:54.706 align:middle
that markets and participants
have different views

33:54.706 --> 33:58.926 align:middle
on the evolution of
economic conditions.

33:58.956 --> 34:03.196 align:middle
For example, I think I'd note

34:03.276 --> 34:07.866 align:middle
that when the Committee
participants write

34:07.986 --> 34:11.276 align:middle
down their forecast for
the federal funds rate,

34:12.016 --> 34:17.156 align:middle
they are showing
the funds rate path

34:17.286 --> 34:19.686 align:middle
that they consider most likely.

34:20.316 --> 34:25.206 align:middle
Their economic forecasts
are of the conditions

34:25.476 --> 34:28.416 align:middle
that they think are
the most likely ones.

34:28.416 --> 34:31.776 align:middle
You don't see the full range
of possibilities there.

34:32.156 --> 34:35.686 align:middle
And the path for the
funds rate is the path

34:35.686 --> 34:39.116 align:middle
that each individual
thinks is most likely.

34:39.996 --> 34:43.076 align:middle
Market participants,
understanding

34:43.076 --> 34:46.936 align:middle
that there are a range of
possible outcomes, with upside

34:46.936 --> 34:48.986 align:middle
and downside possibilities,

34:49.456 --> 34:52.816 align:middle
are doing something
slightly different, I think,

34:52.816 --> 34:56.946 align:middle
when they're determining
market prices.

34:57.386 --> 35:00.796 align:middle
They are taking into
account the possibility

35:00.796 --> 35:04.196 align:middle
that there can be
different economic outcomes,

35:04.426 --> 35:09.126 align:middle
including-even if they're
not very likely-ones

35:09.126 --> 35:13.706 align:middle
in which outcomes will be
characterized by low inflation

35:13.706 --> 35:16.486 align:middle
or low growth and
the appropriate path

35:16.486 --> 35:17.796 align:middle
of rates will be low.

35:18.206 --> 35:21.056 align:middle
So, differences in probabilities

35:21.056 --> 35:25.546 align:middle
of different outcomes
can explain part of that.

35:25.546 --> 35:27.606 align:middle
We, you know, want to learn,

35:27.606 --> 35:31.606 align:middle
we-market participants may have
different views on the economy

35:31.606 --> 35:36.226 align:middle
than the Committee does, and
that's something we want to try

35:36.226 --> 35:39.126 align:middle
to infer and learn market views,

35:39.306 --> 35:42.376 align:middle
in part from information
of this type.

35:42.906 --> 35:46.866 align:middle
What I can say is
that it is important

35:46.866 --> 35:49.006 align:middle
for market participants

35:49.606 --> 35:54.366 align:middle
to understand what our likely
response or reaction function is

35:54.366 --> 35:59.266 align:middle
to the data, and our job is to
try to communicate as clearly

35:59.266 --> 36:03.576 align:middle
as we can the way in which
our policy stance will depend

36:03.576 --> 36:08.086 align:middle
on the data, and I
promise to try to do that.

36:08.656 --> 36:14.476 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Hi, Ylan
Mui, Washington Post.

36:14.476 --> 36:17.526 align:middle
My question is about
the new exit principles.

36:17.526 --> 36:20.896 align:middle
You guys say that you don't
plan to end reinvestments

36:20.986 --> 36:23.676 align:middle
until after the first rate hike.

36:23.926 --> 36:25.636 align:middle
Can you give us a
little bit of a sense

36:25.716 --> 36:28.286 align:middle
of what are the conditions
you're going to be looking

36:28.286 --> 36:30.966 align:middle
for when you eventually begin
to end the reinvestments?

36:30.966 --> 36:33.586 align:middle
It sounds like tapering
the reinvestments is also

36:33.586 --> 36:34.396 align:middle
on the table.

36:34.946 --> 36:37.256 align:middle
What might go into your
decision on whether or not

36:37.256 --> 36:39.246 align:middle
to end them altogether,
whether or not to taper them?

36:39.246 --> 36:41.886 align:middle
And do you have a
general timeline

36:41.966 --> 36:43.456 align:middle
for how long you
think it will take

36:43.456 --> 36:46.036 align:middle
to shrink the balance sheet
once you actually start?

36:46.036 --> 36:46.936 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:46.936 --> 36:48.516 align:middle
Okay. All good questions.

36:48.906 --> 36:55.156 align:middle
So, I think the Committee
would-will be focused on-we

36:55.156 --> 36:59.196 align:middle
intend to use the path of
short-term interest rates

36:59.196 --> 37:02.026 align:middle
as our key tool of policy.

37:02.486 --> 37:03.776 align:middle
And, of course,

37:03.776 --> 37:07.936 align:middle
market participants will
be very focused, as we are,

37:07.936 --> 37:11.336 align:middle
on what is the appropriate
timing and pace

37:11.336 --> 37:14.926 align:middle
of interest rate increases
when that time comes.

37:15.416 --> 37:18.356 align:middle
And I think the Committee
would like to feel

37:18.646 --> 37:23.616 align:middle
that it has successfully begun
the normalization process

37:23.886 --> 37:28.926 align:middle
and that we're successfully
communicating with markets

37:28.926 --> 37:33.696 align:middle
about how that process will
be playing out over time.

37:34.046 --> 37:37.126 align:middle
And I think when the
Committee is comfortable

37:37.126 --> 37:40.726 align:middle
that that process is
established, is working well,

37:40.906 --> 37:42.896 align:middle
and we're comfortable
with the outlook,

37:43.216 --> 37:45.326 align:middle
that they will begin the process

37:45.326 --> 37:50.466 align:middle
of ceasing-or possibly
tapering-but eventually

37:50.576 --> 37:52.606 align:middle
ceasing reinvestments.

37:53.466 --> 37:56.256 align:middle
So, we say that it
will depend on economic

37:56.256 --> 37:57.766 align:middle
and financial conditions,

37:57.766 --> 38:01.276 align:middle
but we want to make sure
normalization is successfully

38:01.586 --> 38:02.406 align:middle
under way.

38:03.466 --> 38:08.816 align:middle
If we were only to
shrink our balance sheet

38:09.236 --> 38:16.216 align:middle
by ceasing reinvestments, it
would probably take-to get back

38:16.316 --> 38:18.946 align:middle
to levels of reserve balances

38:18.946 --> 38:22.846 align:middle
that we had before the
crisis-I'm not sure we will go

38:22.846 --> 38:24.946 align:middle
that low, but we've
said that we will try

38:24.946 --> 38:29.876 align:middle
to shrink our balance sheet to
the lowest levels consistent

38:29.876 --> 38:32.726 align:middle
with the efficient and
effective implementation

38:32.726 --> 38:36.426 align:middle
of policy-it could take
to the end of the decade

38:36.426 --> 38:38.836 align:middle
to achieve those levels.

38:40.176 --> 38:41.416 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

38:41.416 --> 38:46.766 align:middle
You said a few moments ago

38:46.766 --> 38:49.006 align:middle
that there is perhaps
some remaining shortfall

38:49.006 --> 38:52.056 align:middle
of labor force participation as
a result of cyclical factors.

38:52.436 --> 38:54.696 align:middle
This seems consistent with a
recent paper by some members

38:54.696 --> 38:57.456 align:middle
of your staff finding that labor
force participation is unlikely

38:57.456 --> 38:57.956 align:middle
to recover.

38:58.226 --> 39:00.886 align:middle
Is that now the "house" view
that slack essentially consists

39:00.886 --> 39:03.956 align:middle
of unemployment plus part-time
workers who want full-time work,

39:03.956 --> 39:06.596 align:middle
and that labor force
participation is basically

39:06.596 --> 39:07.376 align:middle
out of the equation?

39:07.846 --> 39:10.896 align:middle
And the second question-in the
statement of exit principles,

39:10.896 --> 39:12.746 align:middle
you said that the
Committee will act as soon

39:12.746 --> 39:14.376 align:middle
as economic conditions warrant.

39:14.776 --> 39:16.926 align:middle
There had been an idea in
circulation at some point

39:16.956 --> 39:19.226 align:middle
that you might stay lower
for longer as a means

39:19.226 --> 39:20.576 align:middle
of compensating for some

39:20.576 --> 39:22.266 align:middle
of the damage done
during the recession.

39:22.576 --> 39:24.806 align:middle
Is this an indication that
that debate has been settled,

39:24.806 --> 39:26.216 align:middle
and that that idea
is off the table?

39:26.316 --> 39:26.926 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:26.926 --> 39:27.176 align:middle
I'm sorry.

39:27.176 --> 39:28.796 align:middle
Just remind me, what
was the first question?

39:28.996 --> 39:31.356 align:middle
The first question-
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

39:31.356 --> 39:32.756 align:middle
The first question
was- CHAIR YELLEN.

39:32.936 --> 39:34.546 align:middle
Please. BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

39:34.846 --> 39:39.076 align:middle
The first question was, has
the labor force participation-

39:39.336 --> 39:39.696 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:39.696 --> 39:46.676 align:middle
Ah, labor force participation-
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

39:47.056 --> 39:50.226 align:middle
-been removed from
the slack equation?

39:50.226 --> 39:51.096 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:51.096 --> 39:53.256 align:middle
So the recent Brookings paper

39:53.256 --> 39:56.466 align:middle
by the Fed economists clearly
indicates, and I said this

39:56.466 --> 39:58.156 align:middle
at Jackson Hole, that there
are structural reasons,

39:58.156 --> 40:03.906 align:middle
particularly demographics
but not only demographics,

40:03.906 --> 40:07.546 align:middle
why labor force participation
should be expected

40:07.546 --> 40:08.246 align:middle
to decline over time.

40:08.246 --> 40:11.016 align:middle
And I agree with that,
and I believe that most

40:11.016 --> 40:14.366 align:middle
of my colleagues would
endorse that as well.

40:14.366 --> 40:16.326 align:middle
However, they did indicate
that, in the paper,

40:16.326 --> 40:19.726 align:middle
that they see some
remaining cyclical shortfall.

40:20.286 --> 40:24.196 align:middle
By one technique that they
used, they placed the estimate

40:24.196 --> 40:28.286 align:middle
at 1/4 percent, but
by another technique

40:28.286 --> 40:30.476 align:middle
that they used it could
be, I believe they said,

40:30.476 --> 40:32.076 align:middle
as large as 1 percent.

40:32.076 --> 40:34.386 align:middle
And so my own personal view is

40:34.436 --> 40:36.276 align:middle
that there is some
cyclical shortfall,

40:36.276 --> 40:37.836 align:middle
and something certainly-probably
within that range.

40:37.916 --> 40:41.746 align:middle
But nevertheless, it's a
meaningful cyclical shortfall.

40:41.746 --> 40:45.836 align:middle
It's not completely-so I'm
giving my own personal view,

40:45.836 --> 40:50.146 align:middle
not a Committee assessment-that,
you know,

40:50.146 --> 40:54.626 align:middle
I see the flat-and given the
underlying downward trend

40:54.626 --> 40:58.926 align:middle
in labor force participation, we
might interpret the flattening

40:58.926 --> 41:04.296 align:middle
out of labor force participation
over the last year as showing

41:04.296 --> 41:07.756 align:middle
that that cyclical component
has diminished somewhat.

41:07.986 --> 41:10.126 align:middle
I think there is
something that remains,

41:10.476 --> 41:13.866 align:middle
but eventually I would certainly
agree with the authors,

41:14.176 --> 41:15.846 align:middle
we should expect over time

41:15.846 --> 41:18.886 align:middle
to see labor force
participation declining.

41:18.886 --> 41:22.416 align:middle
And then, let's see, the second
piece was- BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

41:22.466 --> 41:23.186 align:middle
Is the debate

41:25.346 --> 41:26.486 align:middle
about lower-for-longer
essentially over?

41:26.486 --> 41:26.726 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

41:26.726 --> 41:32.246 align:middle
Well, you know, we stayed
low for a very long time.

41:32.246 --> 41:40.096 align:middle
We have been at zero for a very
long time and below the levels

41:40.096 --> 41:44.546 align:middle
that some common policy rules
would now be suggesting,

41:44.966 --> 41:48.426 align:middle
given the level of
unemployment and inflation.

41:49.096 --> 41:52.056 align:middle
So the recovery has
been very slow.

41:52.056 --> 41:56.126 align:middle
We've also been doing
unconventional policies,

41:56.126 --> 41:57.766 align:middle
of course, buying assets.

41:58.206 --> 42:02.356 align:middle
And in a general sense, I
think we have been lower

42:02.436 --> 42:04.766 align:middle
for longer than-if you complete

42:04.766 --> 42:09.796 align:middle
that sentence-than many standard
policy rules would suggest.

42:09.796 --> 42:13.896 align:middle
So, in a sense, that is a
policy that we have had.

42:14.316 --> 42:19.696 align:middle
And once we decide it's
appropriate to begin

42:19.696 --> 42:25.296 align:middle
to normalize policy and to
raise the level of our target

42:25.296 --> 42:29.216 align:middle
for short-term interest rates,
it would still take some time

42:29.336 --> 42:33.806 align:middle
for rates to get back
to levels-you can see

42:33.806 --> 42:37.266 align:middle
in our projection that
by the end of 2017,

42:37.266 --> 42:41.906 align:middle
the participants
are, on average,

42:42.196 --> 42:46.726 align:middle
projecting that rates will reach
the levels they consider normal

42:46.726 --> 42:47.896 align:middle
in the longer run.

42:48.196 --> 42:52.336 align:middle
And, similarly, we could make a
similar statement with respect

42:52.336 --> 42:57.646 align:middle
to where the funds rate
would stand relative

42:57.646 --> 42:59.606 align:middle
to the recommendations of rules.

42:59.866 --> 43:06.596 align:middle
So that would take some time to
return to those kinds of levels.

43:08.136 --> 43:09.796 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

43:12.286 --> 43:12.566 align:middle
Thank you.

43:13.106 --> 43:15.146 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

43:15.676 --> 43:19.186 align:middle
Madam Chair, there were two
dissents from today's decision.

43:19.516 --> 43:22.126 align:middle
I'd kind of like to
get your thinking

43:22.126 --> 43:23.686 align:middle
on how you treat dissents.

43:23.686 --> 43:28.226 align:middle
I think in Chairman Bernanke's
eight years, the largest number

43:28.226 --> 43:29.506 align:middle
of dissents was three.

43:29.916 --> 43:33.396 align:middle
Do you see two dissents as a
yellow-warning flashing light

43:33.716 --> 43:36.736 align:middle
that policy may need to be
moderated down the road?

43:36.736 --> 43:39.556 align:middle
How should market
participants read the dissents?

43:40.106 --> 43:40.696 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:41.016 --> 43:44.026 align:middle
Well, I think it's very natural

43:44.156 --> 43:47.706 align:middle
that the Committee should
have a range of opinion

43:48.096 --> 43:53.676 align:middle
about a decision as crucial as
what is the right time to begin

43:53.676 --> 43:59.316 align:middle
to normalize policy,
and we do have a range

43:59.316 --> 44:00.986 align:middle
of views in the Committee.

44:01.366 --> 44:07.496 align:middle
I don't consider two dissents to
be an abnormally large number.

44:07.816 --> 44:14.006 align:middle
Presidents Plosser and Fisher
have been quite clear in all

44:14.006 --> 44:17.566 align:middle
of their speeches
recently in stating

44:17.566 --> 44:20.006 align:middle
that they think the
time has come

44:20.726 --> 44:22.876 align:middle
to begin normalizing policy.

44:23.356 --> 44:29.116 align:middle
I think they, perhaps, have some
concerns that if we don't begin

44:29.116 --> 44:32.756 align:middle
to do so soon that
inflation will pick

44:32.756 --> 44:37.336 align:middle
up above levels we-that they
would consider desirable,

44:37.766 --> 44:42.226 align:middle
or that they have some
financial stability concerns.

44:42.816 --> 44:47.076 align:middle
But the Committee
adopted today's statement

44:47.076 --> 44:52.306 align:middle
by an overwhelming majority,
and I don't consider the level

44:52.306 --> 44:56.486 align:middle
of dissent to be
surprising or very abnormal.

44:57.896 --> 45:03.706 align:middle
GREG IP. Greg Ip
of The Economist.

45:03.866 --> 45:04.886 align:middle
There's been another downgrade

45:04.886 --> 45:07.016 align:middle
in your near-term growth
forecast and a downgrade

45:07.016 --> 45:08.686 align:middle
in your unemployment
rate path forecast.

45:09.146 --> 45:12.096 align:middle
Does it appear that potential
is much lower than you thought,

45:12.096 --> 45:14.366 align:middle
and therefore slack is closing
more quickly than you thought?

45:14.366 --> 45:16.766 align:middle
This seems to be a persistent
pattern to your forecasts.

45:17.026 --> 45:19.276 align:middle
Secondarily, how concerned
are you about events in Europe

45:19.276 --> 45:20.496 align:middle
and especially the
recent decline

45:20.496 --> 45:21.596 align:middle
in inflation expectations there?

45:21.596 --> 45:22.426 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

45:23.116 --> 45:27.546 align:middle
So there's been a little
bit of downgrading, I think,

45:27.546 --> 45:33.466 align:middle
even this time in the
longer-run normal growth rates

45:33.806 --> 45:38.286 align:middle
that Committee participants
have written down.

45:38.956 --> 45:44.916 align:middle
You are certainly right in
saying that over a number

45:44.916 --> 45:50.696 align:middle
of years now, there's been
a pattern of forecast errors

45:50.696 --> 45:55.636 align:middle
in which either we've
been on track with respect

45:55.636 --> 45:58.066 align:middle
to unemployment or
unemployment has come

45:58.066 --> 46:02.736 align:middle
down in some cases faster
than we anticipated,

46:02.946 --> 46:08.566 align:middle
and yet growth has pretty
persistently been surprising the

46:08.566 --> 46:10.546 align:middle
Committee to the downside.

46:10.816 --> 46:14.586 align:middle
And that is a statement
about productivity growth,

46:14.666 --> 46:16.636 align:middle
which has been pretty
disappointing.

46:17.046 --> 46:21.436 align:middle
So we have had downward
revisions in the level

46:21.436 --> 46:25.616 align:middle
of potential output and to some
extent, at least for a time,

46:25.616 --> 46:29.256 align:middle
in the projected pace of growth.

46:29.656 --> 46:32.736 align:middle
So that tension has been there.

46:32.926 --> 46:36.786 align:middle
There are a range of views
about long-run growth.

46:36.786 --> 46:39.986 align:middle
A lot of people are
writing about this topic.

46:40.316 --> 46:46.006 align:middle
I think the Committee's
longer-run estimates are neither

46:46.286 --> 46:49.986 align:middle
at the most pessimistic end
nor the most optimistic end.

46:54.696 --> 46:55.956 align:middle
GREG IP. I had a
question about Europe.

46:55.956 --> 46:56.376 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:56.376 --> 46:57.236 align:middle
Oh, about Europe.

46:57.236 --> 46:58.166 align:middle
Well, I mean, you know,

46:58.456 --> 47:02.636 align:middle
certainly we have
discussed the outlook

47:03.226 --> 47:08.656 align:middle
for Europe-the very
low level of inflation

47:09.136 --> 47:14.466 align:middle
that they have seen recently
and the decline that they saw

47:14.466 --> 47:18.576 align:middle
in inflationary expectations,
in the slow pace of growth.

47:19.196 --> 47:24.226 align:middle
It is one of a number of
risks to the global economy,

47:24.226 --> 47:29.396 align:middle
and we certainly hope that
they will be successful

47:29.396 --> 47:34.446 align:middle
in seeing the pace of growth
and inflation pick up,

47:34.446 --> 47:37.106 align:middle
and I think that will be
good for the global economy

47:37.106 --> 47:39.466 align:middle
and the United States.

47:42.586 --> 47:44.536 align:middle
ROBIN HARDING.

47:44.536 --> 47:46.436 align:middle
Robin Harding from
the Financial Times.

47:46.756 --> 47:49.546 align:middle
Madam Chair, I want to come
back to the forward guidance.

47:49.626 --> 47:50.916 align:middle
Quite a large part

47:50.916 --> 47:53.586 align:middle
of the Committee has recently
criticized the guidance

47:53.586 --> 47:54.966 align:middle
as being calendar based.

47:55.336 --> 47:57.006 align:middle
But if I understood
your comments earlier,

47:57.046 --> 47:58.896 align:middle
that's not correct.

47:59.926 --> 48:01.646 align:middle
How should people understand it?

48:01.646 --> 48:03.876 align:middle
And if it doesn't have
a defined meaning,

48:04.456 --> 48:06.306 align:middle
what purpose is it
actually serving?

48:06.566 --> 48:09.056 align:middle
And do you expect to have to
revisit it in the near future?

48:09.296 --> 48:09.566 align:middle
Thank you.

48:10.986 --> 48:11.446 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:11.446 --> 48:14.906 align:middle
So we are constantly discussing
forward guidance and thinking

48:14.906 --> 48:18.096 align:middle
about whether it's appropriate
and how to revise it.

48:18.536 --> 48:20.856 align:middle
And we did do a major overhaul

48:20.856 --> 48:23.276 align:middle
of our forward guidance
in March.

48:23.876 --> 48:29.336 align:middle
I think the Committee
participants who have spoken

48:29.336 --> 48:33.936 align:middle
out on this topic
recently want to make sure

48:34.366 --> 48:37.556 align:middle
that we have the
flexibility-that the Committee

48:37.786 --> 48:43.136 align:middle
has the flexibility to respond
to unfolding developments.

48:43.906 --> 48:48.936 align:middle
They want to make sure that
if progress really does turn

48:48.936 --> 48:53.876 align:middle
out to be faster than
we have-we would expect,

48:54.346 --> 48:57.436 align:middle
that the Committee
will be in a position

48:57.436 --> 49:02.106 align:middle
to start sooner tightening
monetary policy.

49:02.576 --> 49:06.386 align:middle
They do not want to be
locked into something

49:06.386 --> 49:07.726 align:middle
that the markets see

49:07.846 --> 49:11.686 align:middle
as a calendar-based
and firm commitment.

49:12.006 --> 49:17.216 align:middle
And so they want to emphasize
data dependence of our policy

49:17.376 --> 49:20.346 align:middle
and make sure that we have
appropriate flexibility.

49:21.016 --> 49:23.356 align:middle
But I agree with that.

49:23.356 --> 49:29.626 align:middle
And, as I said earlier, I think
we do not have any mechanical

49:29.626 --> 49:32.696 align:middle
interpretation that
applies to this.

49:33.096 --> 49:35.846 align:middle
It, of course, gives
an impression

49:35.846 --> 49:38.276 align:middle
about what we think
will be appropriate,

49:38.776 --> 49:41.996 align:middle
but there is no mechanical
interpretation.

49:42.226 --> 49:44.786 align:middle
And I've said repeatedly,
and I want to say again,

49:44.786 --> 49:47.166 align:middle
that if events surprise us,

49:47.496 --> 49:51.656 align:middle
and we're moving more
quickly toward our objectives,

49:51.656 --> 49:57.266 align:middle
and the Committee sees a need to
move sooner or later depending

49:57.266 --> 50:02.566 align:middle
on what the data is, that we
do feel-I do feel we have the

50:02.566 --> 50:05.276 align:middle
flexibility to move.

50:05.276 --> 50:07.946 align:middle
And it is important for
markets to understand

50:08.326 --> 50:13.806 align:middle
that there is uncertainty, and
this statement is not some sort

50:13.806 --> 50:19.166 align:middle
of firm promise about a
particular amount of time.

50:21.056 --> 50:21.656 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

50:22.056 --> 50:26.296 align:middle
Good afternoon, Chair Yellen.

50:26.946 --> 50:29.606 align:middle
Getting back to the one aspect

50:29.606 --> 50:32.036 align:middle
of the forward guidance
is the statement,

50:32.556 --> 50:36.756 align:middle
which you've reiterated about
the funds rate probably needing

50:36.756 --> 50:39.556 align:middle
to stay below normal
for some time

50:39.556 --> 50:42.486 align:middle
after achieving
mandate-consistent levels

50:42.776 --> 50:44.986 align:middle
on unemployment,
inflation, and so forth.

50:45.666 --> 50:48.666 align:middle
The SEP assessments

50:48.666 --> 50:52.586 align:middle
of appropriate funds rate levels
show the funds rate getting

50:52.586 --> 50:58.406 align:middle
up to that 3.75 percent normal
level at the end of 2017.

50:58.906 --> 51:02.576 align:middle
If you look at the SEP
projections of unemployment,

51:02.576 --> 51:05.716 align:middle
inflation, and so forth,
they seem to get back

51:05.716 --> 51:09.956 align:middle
to those mandate-consistent
levels by the end

51:09.956 --> 51:12.816 align:middle
of 2016, if not much sooner.

51:12.816 --> 51:17.786 align:middle
So what is the justification
for waiting that much longer

51:18.106 --> 51:21.706 align:middle
to get back to normal,
particularly when you have

51:21.706 --> 51:24.766 align:middle
such a large balance
sheet that you intend

51:24.766 --> 51:26.856 align:middle
to reduce only gradually?

51:26.856 --> 51:29.626 align:middle
Is there a danger of
getting behind the curve?

51:30.106 --> 51:34.636 align:middle
And, secondly, can I just ask,
can you envision a time when,

51:34.636 --> 51:38.046 align:middle
to reduce reserve pressures,
you may have to resort

51:38.426 --> 51:41.336 align:middle
to asset sales that you
don't anticipate doing now?

51:41.336 --> 51:42.316 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

51:42.996 --> 51:49.006 align:middle
So on the first question of
"some time" before rates return

51:49.006 --> 51:52.206 align:middle
to normal levels,
as I mentioned,

51:52.246 --> 51:57.236 align:middle
you can see in the SEP
that by the end of 2017,

51:57.586 --> 52:00.876 align:middle
many participants
are anticipating

52:01.236 --> 52:06.256 align:middle
that rates will return to what
they think are normal longer-run

52:06.256 --> 52:09.886 align:middle
levels, but the economy,
in their view,

52:10.276 --> 52:15.346 align:middle
will have probably gotten back
to normal levels of unemployment

52:15.556 --> 52:20.246 align:middle
and near-normal levels of
inflation sometime in 2016.

52:20.646 --> 52:24.616 align:middle
So that looks like
a year or more

52:24.616 --> 52:28.396 align:middle
in which rates would be below
normal longer-run levels.

52:29.836 --> 52:34.606 align:middle
We asked participants why they
hold the views that they do

52:34.606 --> 52:38.826 align:middle
about appropriate policy,
and there are a number

52:38.826 --> 52:42.216 align:middle
of different explanations
that participants give.

52:42.736 --> 52:48.636 align:middle
But a common view on this is
that there have been a variety

52:48.636 --> 52:54.606 align:middle
of headwinds resulting from the
crisis that have slowed growth,

52:55.146 --> 52:59.956 align:middle
led to a sluggish
recovery from the crisis,

53:00.366 --> 53:04.996 align:middle
and that these headwinds
will dissipate only slowly,

53:04.996 --> 53:08.866 align:middle
that they are dissipating-an
example would be the fact

53:08.866 --> 53:14.286 align:middle
that mortgage credit really is,
at this point, available really

53:14.286 --> 53:16.276 align:middle
to those with pristine credit.

53:16.556 --> 53:20.156 align:middle
Credit conditions there
are abnormally tight.

53:20.576 --> 53:26.466 align:middle
Another thing that we see is
that households' expectations

53:26.466 --> 53:32.156 align:middle
about their likely income paths
remain quite depressed relative

53:32.206 --> 53:33.776 align:middle
to pre-crisis levels.

53:34.146 --> 53:37.586 align:middle
That's something that may be
holding back consumer spending.

53:38.076 --> 53:44.906 align:middle
So the view would be that those
forces will dissipate over time,

53:44.946 --> 53:46.536 align:middle
but only very gradually.

53:46.536 --> 53:49.806 align:middle
In addition, we have had
slow productivity growth,

53:50.216 --> 53:56.376 align:middle
and a slow pace of potential
output likely depresses the pace

53:56.376 --> 53:57.876 align:middle
of investment spending.

53:58.216 --> 54:02.286 align:middle
And so, those are some of the
things that participants mention

54:02.686 --> 54:06.056 align:middle
as why it will take
some time to get back.

54:06.226 --> 54:11.386 align:middle
So the story is, it's not that
the Fed is behind the curve

54:11.776 --> 54:17.256 align:middle
in failing to return the
funds rate to normal levels

54:17.256 --> 54:19.226 align:middle
when the economy is recovered.

54:19.536 --> 54:25.466 align:middle
It is rather that, in order to
achieve such a recovery in 2016

54:25.466 --> 54:29.786 align:middle
or by the end, that it's
necessary and appropriate

54:29.786 --> 54:33.146 align:middle
to have a somewhat more
accommodative policy

54:33.456 --> 54:36.696 align:middle
than would be normal in the
absence of those headwinds.

54:37.366 --> 54:41.336 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

54:42.096 --> 54:43.436 align:middle
Peter Barnes of Fox
Business, ma'am.

54:43.826 --> 54:46.256 align:middle
I would like to follow up on
Greg's question about Europe,

54:46.256 --> 54:49.236 align:middle
because tomorrow Scotland
is going to be voting

54:49.406 --> 54:51.346 align:middle
on independence from
Great Britain,

54:51.476 --> 54:56.626 align:middle
and there's some concern
that if it does vote to break

54:57.076 --> 55:00.276 align:middle
from Great Britain, that
this could cause some turmoil

55:00.276 --> 55:02.626 align:middle
in global financial markets
and the global economy.

55:03.066 --> 55:04.816 align:middle
Are you concerned about that?

55:04.816 --> 55:08.016 align:middle
Do you see any impact
if Scotland does vote

55:08.016 --> 55:10.866 align:middle
for independence on the
European economy and potentially

55:10.866 --> 55:12.006 align:middle
on the American economy?

55:12.006 --> 55:14.086 align:middle
And, if so, is the
Fed doing anything

55:14.086 --> 55:16.256 align:middle
in preparation for
that possibility?

55:16.256 --> 55:16.556 align:middle
Thank you.

55:16.556 --> 55:16.886 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

55:16.886 --> 55:20.326 align:middle
Well, Scottish voters are about
to go to the polls tomorrow,

55:20.326 --> 55:24.266 align:middle
and they've had a good
debate about this topic.

55:24.266 --> 55:27.326 align:middle
And in light of that,
I really don't want

55:27.436 --> 55:29.316 align:middle
to weigh in on this today.

55:29.386 --> 55:31.286 align:middle
PEDRO DA COSTA.

55:35.346 --> 55:35.726 align:middle
Thank you.

55:35.786 --> 55:37.606 align:middle
Pedro da Costa with
Dow Jones Newswires.

55:37.996 --> 55:40.526 align:middle
My question is about
your particular views

55:40.526 --> 55:44.116 align:middle
about whether a gradual
approach to tightening is better

55:44.226 --> 55:46.366 align:middle
than a more aggressive
and less predictable one,

55:46.366 --> 55:49.756 align:middle
because there was some
discussion about-internally

55:49.756 --> 55:52.676 align:middle
and externally, about whether
it was the Fed's predictability

55:53.076 --> 55:58.426 align:middle
in the 2004-ish period that
kind of created the conditions

55:58.426 --> 56:00.386 align:middle
of complacency that led
to the housing bubble.

56:00.386 --> 56:03.656 align:middle
So I wonder if you'd be more
inclined to be gradualist

56:03.656 --> 56:06.986 align:middle
in your approach
and more transparent

56:06.986 --> 56:08.646 align:middle
in outlining future moves

56:08.646 --> 56:11.616 align:middle
or whether you think keeping
the market guessing-there's some

56:11.616 --> 56:12.266 align:middle
value to that.

56:12.466 --> 56:13.956 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

56:14.856 --> 56:18.136 align:middle
You know, this is something
the Committee is going to have

56:18.216 --> 56:22.676 align:middle
to discuss when the time
comes to normalize policy.

56:23.086 --> 56:27.696 align:middle
Looking back on the period, the
run-up to the financial crisis,

56:28.086 --> 56:32.146 align:middle
I don't think, by any
means, "measured pace"

56:32.146 --> 56:34.796 align:middle
and the very predictable pace

56:34.796 --> 56:40.046 align:middle
of 25 basis points per meeting
explains why we had a financial

56:40.046 --> 56:43.856 align:middle
crisis, but it may have
diminished volatility

56:43.856 --> 56:48.426 align:middle
and been a small
contributing factor,

56:48.706 --> 56:52.536 align:middle
and the Committee will have
to think about how to do this.

56:52.946 --> 56:57.966 align:middle
I think many people in the
aftermath of that episode think

56:58.256 --> 56:59.616 align:middle
that somewhat less

56:59.616 --> 57:03.096 align:middle
of a mechanical pace
would perhaps be better,

57:03.096 --> 57:05.216 align:middle
but this is a matter
that we will,

57:05.426 --> 57:21.106 align:middle
in due time, have to discuss.

